    "lem-nrqmx-finite-mode-fock-schr-identification": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-hilbert-direct-sum-construction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert\nspace with inner product linear in the first argument\n([[def-hilbert-space]], [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]). Let\n$H^{\\otimes 0}:=\\mathbb C$, $H^{\\otimes 1}:=H$ and\n$H^{\\otimes(n+1)}:=H^{\\otimes n}\\widehat\\otimes H$ be the completed tensor\npowers with elementary tensors $u_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes u_n$\n([[lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction]]); for $n\\ge 1$ let\n$P_+^{(n)}\\in\\mathcal B(H^{\\otimes n})$ be the symmetric projection of\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections]] and set\n$P_+^{(0)}:=\\mathrm{id}_{\\mathbb C}$. Write $\\operatorname{ran}P_+^{(n)}$ for\nits range and define",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-creation-contraction-bounds",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a\ncomplex Hilbert space with inner product linear in the first argument\n([[def-hilbert-space]], [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]), with\ncompleted tensor powers $H^{\\otimes n}$, symmetric projections $P_+^{(n)}$,\nFock space $F(H)$ and finite-particle space $F_0$ as in\n[[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-hilbert-direct-sum-construction]] and\n[[lem-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-fock-sector-projections]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-nrqm-oscillator-multidimensional-oscillator-completeness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The functions $g_N$ have smooth product representatives from the recursive construction. These representatives are unique: a continuous difference that is nonzero at a point remains nonzero on a ball of positive Lebesgue measure. The coordinate derivatives in (b) and (c) are taken on these representatives and their scaled products.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-nrqm-oscillator-hermite-recurrence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $L^2=L^2(\\mathbb R,dy)$ be the complex Lebesgue space with inner product $\\langle\\varphi,\\psi\\rangle=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\varphi(y)\\overline{\\psi(y)}\\,dy$ linear in the first argument ([[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]], [[def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions]]); this pairing makes $L^2$ a complex Hilbert space by [[lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space]]. For complex-valued functions, smoothness and derivatives use the componentwise convention of [[def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions]]. Write $g_0$ for the function $g_0(y):=\\pi^{-1/4}e^{-y^{2}/2}$ and also for its class in $L^2$. Put",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-hilbert-direct-sum-construction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert\nspace with inner product linear in the first argument\n([[def-hilbert-space]], [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]). Let\n$H^{\\otimes 0}:=\\mathbb C$, $H^{\\otimes 1}:=H$ and\n$H^{\\otimes(n+1)}:=H^{\\otimes n}\\widehat\\otimes H$ be the completed tensor\npowers with elementary tensors $u_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes u_n$\n([[lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction]]); for $n\\ge 1$ let\n$P_+^{(n)}\\in\\mathcal B(H^{\\otimes n})$ be the symmetric projection of\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections]] and set\n$P_+^{(0)}:=\\mathrm{id}_{\\mathbb C}$. Write $\\operatorname{ran}P_+^{(n)}$ for\nits range and define",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-creation-contraction-bounds",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a\ncomplex Hilbert space with inner product linear in the first argument\n([[def-hilbert-space]], [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]), with\ncompleted tensor powers $H^{\\otimes n}$, symmetric projections $P_+^{(n)}$,\nFock space $F(H)$ and finite-particle space $F_0$ as in\n[[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-hilbert-direct-sum-construction]] and\n[[lem-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-fock-sector-projections]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For fixed $n$, the symmetric tensors are the range of the averaging\nprojection on $(\\mathbb C^r)^{\\otimes n}$. The elementary tensors in the basis\n$e_1,\\ldots,e_r$ span the tensor power, so their averages span that range.\nTwo such averages with different occupation vectors are orthogonal, because\nat least one tensor slot has orthogonal basis vectors in every paired term.\nFor occupation vector $N$ the squared norm of the averaged tensor is\n$\\prod_jn_j!/n!$ by counting the $\\prod_jn_j!$ permutations that preserve the\nordered occupation word among $n!$ permutations. Multiplying by\n$\\sqrt{n!/\\prod_jn_j!}$ normalizes it. The CCR in [F2], applied successively\nto the vacuum, show that this normalized vector is exactly $|N\\rangle$;\ntherefore the vectors $|N\\rangle$ are an orthonormal basis of each sector.\nThe sector direct sum in [F1] makes their union a complete orthonormal family\nof Fock space.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The target family $(g_N)$ is complete orthonormal by [F3]. Hence the\nlinear map $|N\\rangle\\mapsto g_N$ preserves norms on finite linear\ncombinations, extends by completion to an isometry on Fock space, and is\nsurjective because its range contains the complete family $(g_N)$ and is\nclosed. Uniqueness follows from density of the finite span.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "On a basis vector $|N\\rangle$, the ladder formulas [F2] and Hermite\nrecurrences [F3] give\n$$\\mathcal U a_j\\mathcal U^{-1}g_N=\\sqrt{n_j}g_{N-e_j}\n=\\frac{x_j+\\partial_{x_j}}{\\sqrt2}g_N,$$\nand\n$$\\mathcal U a_j^*\\mathcal U^{-1}g_N=\\sqrt{n_j+1}g_{N+e_j}\n=\\frac{x_j-\\partial_{x_j}}{\\sqrt2}g_N.$$\nLinearity proves these identities on the finite span. Adding and subtracting\nthe two identities gives the displayed $Q_j,P_j$ formulas there. The formulas\nare only asserted on this core, where all terms are defined.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
