    "lem-nrqmx-finite-symplectic-generator-factorization": {
      "citations": [],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Each listed matrix is symplectic by direct multiplication with $J_0$:\n$U_X^TJ_0U_X=J_0$ iff $X=X^T$, $L_Y^TJ_0L_Y=J_0$ iff $Y=Y^T$, and\n$D_A^TJ_0D_A=J_0$. A quarter-turn on a subset is itself a block diagonal\nproduct of two-dimensional matrices $\\begin{psmallmatrix}0&-1\\\\1&0\\end{psmallmatrix}$\nand identities, so is symplectic; on one selected coordinate it equals the\nproduct $U_{-1}L_1U_{-1}$ in that coordinate. Thus all proposed generators\nare symplectic.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "First suppose the upper-left block $A$ is invertible. The block equations\nin [F1] imply $A^{-1}B=(A^{-1}B)^T$ and $CA^{-1}=(CA^{-1})^T$ by multiplying\n$AB^T=BA^T$ and $A^TC=C^TA$ by the relevant inverses. They also give\n$D=A^{-T}+CA^{-1}B$ from $A^TD-C^TB=I$. Therefore direct block\nmultiplication yields\n$$S=L_{CA^{-1}}D_AU_{A^{-1}B},$$\nwhich is the required factorization in this case.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "For arbitrary $A$, let $k=\\operatorname{rank}A$. Choose invertible real\nmatrices $L,R$ with $LAR=\\operatorname{diag}(I_k,0)$, using row and column\nreduction, and replace $S$ by $S'=D_LSD_R$. This replacement is symplectic.\nWrite its upper-right block as $B'$ and partition rows and columns after the\nfirst $k$. The identity $A'B'^T=B'A'^T$ in [F1], with\n$A'=\\operatorname{diag}(I_k,0)$, forces the lower-left rectangular block of\n$B'$ to vanish. Since the first $r$ rows of $S'$ are independent, the bottom\n$r-k$ rows of $B'$ restricted to its last $r-k$ columns form an invertible\nmatrix $B_{22}$. Right-multiply $S'$ by the inverse partial Fourier rotation\n$F$ on those last coordinates, $(q_j,p_j)\\mapsto(p_j,-q_j)$. It is a product\nof the inverse listed generators. The resulting upper-left block is block upper\ntriangular with diagonal blocks $I_k$ and $-B_{22}$, hence is invertible.\nThis argument also\ncovers $k=0$ and $k=r$ (with the empty block omitted).",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The matrix $S'F$ has invertible upper-left block, so step 1.2 factors it\ninto the stated generators. Since $S=D_L^{-1}(S'F)F^{-1}D_R^{-1}$ and the\ninverses of $D_L,D_R,F$ are of the same listed types, the same is true of\n$S$. This proves the factorization for every symplectic matrix.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement assumes r>=1."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The zero matrix is not symplectic."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 computes the one-mode quarter-turn as a product of shears."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.3 treats singular upper-left blocks."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.3 explicitly covers ranks k=0 and k=r."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The proof uses finite row and column reduction only."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The result is a factorization theorem."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The result is a factorization theorem."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
