    "def-nrqmx-finite-mode-symplectic-data": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-finite-mode-fock-schr-identification",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $r\\ge1$, let $H=\\mathbb C^r$ with orthonormal basis\n$e_1,\\ldots,e_r$, and let $\\mathcal F_s(H)=\\bigoplus_{n\\ge0}H^{\\otimes_s n}$\nbe the bosonic Fock space with vacuum $\\Omega$ and creation and annihilation\noperators $a_j^*,a_j$ from\n[[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-hilbert-direct-sum-construction]] and\n[[lem-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-creation-contraction-bounds]]. For\n$N=(n_1,\\ldots,n_r)\\in\\mathbb N_0^r$, set\n$$|N\\rangle:=\\prod_{j=1}^r\\frac{(a_j^*)^{n_j}}{\\sqrt{n_j!}}\\Omega.$$\nLet $g_N(x)=\\prod_{j=1}^r g_{n_j}(x_j)$ be the normalized product Hermite\nfunction from [[thm-nrqm-oscillator-multidimensional-oscillator-completeness]].\nThen $(|N\\rangle)_N$ and $(g_N)_N$ are complete orthonormal families, and the\nmap $\\mathcal U|N\\rangle=g_N$ extends uniquely to a unitary\n$$\\mathcal U:\\mathcal F_s(\\mathbb C^r)\\longrightarrow L^2(\\mathbb R^r).$$\nOn the algebraic finite-particle span, $\\mathcal U a_j\\mathcal U^{-1}$ and\n$\\mathcal U a_j^*\\mathcal U^{-1}$ are respectively\n$(x_j+\\partial_{x_j})/\\sqrt2$ and $(x_j-\\partial_{x_j})/\\sqrt2$ on product\nHermite functions. In particular the quadratures\n$$Q_j=\\sqrt{\\hbar/2}(a_j+a_j^*),\\qquad\nP_j=-i\\sqrt{\\hbar/2}(a_j-a_j^*)$$\nact there as multiplication by $\\sqrt\\hbar x_j$ and as\n$-i\\sqrt\\hbar\\,\\partial_{x_j}$, respectively. These are core identities;\nthey do not assert that a finite number cutoff preserves the canonical\ncommutation relations.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-closed-adjoint-sector-domains",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $H$ be a\ncomplex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]) with completed tensor powers\n$H^{\\otimes n}$, symmetric projections $P_+^{(n)}$, contraction maps\n$\\Psi_f^{(n)}$, Fock space $F(H)$ and core operators $S_\\pm(f)$ on the\nfinite-particle space $F_0$ as in\n[[lem-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-creation-contraction-bounds]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-creation-contraction-bounds",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a\ncomplex Hilbert space with inner product linear in the first argument\n([[def-hilbert-space]], [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]), with\ncompleted tensor powers $H^{\\otimes n}$, symmetric projections $P_+^{(n)}$,\nFock space $F(H)$ and finite-particle space $F_0$ as in\n[[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-hilbert-direct-sum-construction]] and\n[[lem-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-fock-sector-projections]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-plancherel",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice and let $n\\ge1$. Fourier transformation on Schwartz space extends uniquely to a surjective complex-linear isometry $\\mathcal F_2:L^2(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C)\\to L^2(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C)$. It preserves the first-variable-linear inner product, and hence is unitary.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. The Fourier transform is a topological automorphism of $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^n)$, with $\\mathcal F^2=R$ and $\\mathcal F^{-1}=R\\mathcal F$, where $Rf(x)=f(-x)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-schrodinger-weyl-system-is-regular-and-irreducible",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$$\\big(S_\\lambda(q,p,t)f\\big)(x) =e^{i\\lambda t+i\\lambda p\\cdot(x-q/2)}f(x-q).$$\nThen $S_\\lambda$ is a strongly continuous unitary representation of [[def-heisenberg-group-of-a-symplectic-vector-space]], has central character $t\\mapsto e^{i\\lambda t}$, and its Weyl operators form an irreducible regular Weyl system with phase $e^{-i\\lambda\\omega/2}$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-stone-von-neumann-for-finite-dimensional-weyl-systems",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$$W(v)W(w)=e^{-i\\lambda\\omega(v,w)/2}W(v+w).$$\nThen $W$ is unitarily equivalent to the Schrödinger Weyl system on $L^2(\\mathbb R^n)$, so there is exactly one unitary-equivalence class of irreducible regular Weyl systems with the fixed parameter $\\lambda$. In a symplectic basis the model is\n$$\\big(W_\\lambda(q,p)f\\big)(x)=e^{i\\lambda p\\cdot(x-q/2)}f(x-q),$$\nfor $\\omega((q,p),(q',p'))=q\\cdot p'-p\\cdot q'$. The statement assumes finite-dimensional phase space, irreducibility, strong continuity, and a fixed nonzero central parameter. It makes no claim about algebraic modules, nonregular representations, $\\lambda=0$, or infinitely many modes.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-composites-density-and-scalar-spectral-probability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "(a) A **density operator** on $H$ is a self-adjoint positive trace-class operator $\\rho$ with $\\operatorname{tr}\\rho=1$ ([[def-trace-class-operator]], [[def-trace-of-a-trace-class-operator]], [[def-self-adjoint-positive-unitary-and-normal-operator]]). If $(u_n)_{n\\in J}$ is a finite or countable orthonormal family, with $J$ a finite initial segment of the positive integers or all positive integers, and $w_n\\ge0$ with $\\sum_nw_n=1$, then the sum $\\rho:=\\sum_nw_nT_{u_n,u_n}$ converges in trace norm to a density operator with $\\operatorname{tr}\\rho=\\sum_nw_n=1$. Conversely every density operator $\\rho$ admits such an expansion whose weights are its positive eigenvalues counted with multiplicity and whose vectors are orthonormal eigenvectors; distinct expansions of the same $\\rho$ exist when a positive eigenspace has dimension at least two.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-inner-product-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The first two clauses imply conjugate-linearity in the second argument: $\\langle u,av+bw\\rangle=\\overline a\\langle u,v\\rangle+\\overline b\\langle u,w\\rangle$. This is the linear-first convention of [[def-sesquilinear-and-hermitian-forms-over-a-field-with-involution]].",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "On $L^2(\\mathbb R^r)$, multiplication by real $x_j$ is symmetric. If $g$\nlies in its adjoint domain with adjoint value $h$, testing against compactly\nsupported functions on each box where $x_j$ is bounded gives $h=x_jg$ almost\neverywhere there; the boxes exhaust $\\mathbb R^r$, so $x_jg\\in L^2$ and the\nadjoint domain is exactly $D(M_{x_j})$. Thus $M_{x_j}$ is self-adjoint. The\nfull Fourier transform is unitary by [F2]; conjugating $M_{k_j}$ by it\ndefines the self-adjoint operator $-i\\partial_{x_j}$, since on Schwartz\nfunctions integration by parts gives\n$\\mathcal F(-i\\partial_{x_j}f)=k_j\\mathcal Ff$. Schwartz functions are\na core for multiplication: truncate by smooth compact cutoffs and then\nmollify on the resulting bounded support; dominated convergence controls\n$f$ and $x_jf$, and boundedness of $x_j$ on that support controls the\nmollification error. Fourier invariance of Schwartz space gives the same\ncore assertion for the derivative. Conjugation by $\\mathcal U$ therefore\ndefines the stated self-adjoint quadratures and common core.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "On that core, [F1] gives $Q_j=\\sqrt{\\hbar/2}(a_j+a_j^*)$ and\n$P_j=-i\\sqrt{\\hbar/2}(a_j-a_j^*)$. The creation and annihilation CCR in\n[F1] imply the displayed quadrature commutators by bilinearity; all products are defined on\nthe finite Hermite span. Direct multiplication of the formula for $W$ gives\nthe phase $e^{-i\\hbar(q\\cdot p'-p\\cdot q')/2}$, which is\n$e^{-i\\hbar z^TJz'/2}$ in interleaved notation. Its strong continuity and\nirreducibility follow from [F3] after the parameter-preserving change\n$(q_0,p_0)=(-\\sqrt\\hbar p,q/\\sqrt\\hbar)$ in the root Schrödinger formula.\nThe real symplectic form is unchanged by that map and the central parameter\nis $\\hbar\\ne0$. Applying [F4] proves the representation-equivalence clause\nwith precisely its hypotheses.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The positive trace-class spectral expansion in [F5] has weights summing\nto one. For any eigenvalue $w>0$, changing the orthonormal basis of its\neigenspace changes the sum of squared norms $\\sum\\|R_j\\psi\\|^2$ by a unitary\nfinite-dimensional change of basis when the eigenspace is finite-dimensional;\npositive trace-class compactness makes each nonzero eigenspace finite\ndimensional. The same basis invariance holds for the sesquilinear cross sum\nby polarization, and the zero eigenspace contributes nothing. Cauchy–Schwarz\nthen gives absolute convergence of each first-moment series and of each\ncross-moment series under the stated second-moment bound. Thus the definitions\nof $d,V$ are finite and basis independent; taking the real part makes\n$V_{jk}=V_{kj}\\in\\mathbb R$. Finally, every finite-dimensional cutoff of\nFock space has finite matrix dimension, and for square matrices $A,B$,\n$\\operatorname{tr}(AB-BA)=0$ by expanding the diagonal entries, whereas the\nidentity has positive trace. Hence a finite cutoff cannot satisfy the exact\nCCR $[Q_j,P_j]=i\\hbar I$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
