    "lem-nrqmx-finite-symplectic-implementers": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-schrodinger-weyl-system-is-regular-and-irreducible",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$$\\big(S_\\lambda(q,p,t)f\\big)(x) =e^{i\\lambda t+i\\lambda p\\cdot(x-q/2)}f(x-q).$$\nThen $S_\\lambda$ is a strongly continuous unitary representation of [[def-heisenberg-group-of-a-symplectic-vector-space]], has central character $t\\mapsto e^{i\\lambda t}$, and its Weyl operators form an irreducible regular Weyl system with phase $e^{-i\\lambda\\omega/2}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-finite-symplectic-generator-factorization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $r\\ge1$, $J_0=\\begin{pmatrix}0&I_r\\\\-I_r&0\\end{pmatrix}$, and\n$$\\operatorname{Sp}(2r,\\mathbb R)=\\{S\\in M_{2r}(\\mathbb R):S^TJ_0S=J_0\\}.$$\nEvery $S\\in\\operatorname{Sp}(2r,\\mathbb R)$ is a finite product of matrices\nof the following three types and their inverses:\n$$U_X=\\begin{pmatrix}I&X\\\\0&I\\end{pmatrix},\\quad\nL_Y=\\begin{pmatrix}I&0\\\\Y&I\\end{pmatrix},\\quad\nD_A=\\begin{pmatrix}A&0\\\\0&A^{-T}\\end{pmatrix},$$\nwhere $X,Y$ are real symmetric and $A$ is real invertible. Partial Fourier\nrotations, which act by $(q_j,p_j)\\mapsto(-p_j,q_j)$ on any selected set of\ncoordinates and fix the others, are also products of these matrices.\nIn addition, for every $S$ there is a product $G$ of these generators such\nthat the upper-left block of $SG$ is invertible.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-plancherel",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice and let $n\\ge1$. Fourier transformation on Schwartz space extends uniquely to a surjective complex-linear isometry $\\mathcal F_2:L^2(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C)\\to L^2(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C)$. It preserves the first-variable-linear inner product, and hence is unitary.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-spectral-theorem-for-bounded-normal-operators-pvm-form",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "1. there is a unique regular projection valued measure $E$ on the Borel\n   $\\sigma$-algebra of the nonempty compact set $\\sigma(T)\\subseteq\\mathbb C$\n   such that $\\int z\\,dE(z)=T$ and, equivalently,\n   $$\\Phi_E(f)=f(T)\\qquad\\text{for every continuous }f:\\sigma(T)\\to\\mathbb C,$$\n   where $f(T)$ is the continuous functional calculus of\n   [[thm-continuous-functional-calculus-for-bounded-normal-operators]] and\n   $\\Phi_E$ is the bounded Borel integral of the projection valued measure $E$;\n   this $E$ is the **spectral projection valued measure** of $T$;\n2. conversely, if $\\Lambda\\subseteq\\mathbb C$ is nonempty and compact and $E$ is\n   a regular projection valued measure on the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra of\n   $\\Lambda$, then $T_E:=\\int z\\,dE(z)=\\Phi_E(z)$ is a bounded normal operator\n   with $\\sigma(T_E)\\subseteq\\Lambda$ and $\\|T_E\\|\\le\\max_{z\\in\\Lambda}|z|$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The formula for $W$ is a unitary composition of translation and a\nmodulus-one multiplier; its inverse is $W(-q,-p)$. Direct substitution gives\n$$W(q,p)W(q',p')=e^{-i\\hbar(q\\cdot p'-p\\cdot q')/2}W(q+q',p+p').$$\nFor a real symmetric $X$, the chirp\n$$[C_Xf](x)=e^{ix^TXx/2}f(x)$$\nis unitary, and substitution in the Weyl formula gives\n$C_X^*W(q,p)C_X=W(q+Xp,p)$, the upper shear. For invertible real $A$,\n$$[D_Af](x)=|\\det A|^{1/2}f(Ax)$$\nis unitary by change of variables, and direct substitution gives\n$D_A^*W(q,p)D_A=W(A^{-T}q,Ap)$, an invertible coordinate change.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Let $\\mathcal F$ be the unitary Fourier transform with kernel\n$(2\\pi)^{-r/2}e^{-ik\\cdot x}$, which exists by [F3]. On Schwartz functions,\nchange variables in the integral formula gives\n$$\\mathcal F W(q,p)\\mathcal F^*=W(p,-q).$$\nBoth sides are bounded operators, and Schwartz functions are dense, so this\nidentity holds on all of $L^2$. Applying it to selected coordinates gives\npartial Fourier rotations. Conjugating an upper shear by the full or partial\nFourier rotation gives every lower shear, since the matrix identity is\n$\\begin{psmallmatrix}0&-I\\\\I&0\\end{psmallmatrix}\nU_X\\begin{psmallmatrix}0&I\\\\-I&0\\end{psmallmatrix}=L_{-X}$.\nThus each generator and inverse in [F2] has a unitary Weyl implementer.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Each generator unitary maps Schwartz space onto itself: this follows from\nthe product rule for the smooth quadratic chirp, the chain rule for an\ninvertible linear coordinate change, and Fourier invariance of Schwartz\nspace. On any connected parameter neighborhood on which $A$ remains\ninvertible, the chirp and coordinate formulas vary continuously in every\nSchwartz seminorm; the Fourier factor is fixed. If\n$G_1^*W(z)G_1=W(T_1z)$ and $G_2^*W(z)G_2=W(T_2z)$, then\n$(G_1G_2)^*W(z)(G_1G_2)=W(T_2T_1z)$. Therefore, for any finite factorization\n$T=T_k\\cdots T_1$ supplied by [F2], multiplying the corresponding generator\nunitaries in the order $G_1\\cdots G_k$ gives a unitary $M_T$ with\n$M_T^*W(z)M_T=W(Tz)$ for every label $z$. This proves existence and does not\ninvoke a general metaplectic existence theorem.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Suppose $M_1,M_2$ implement the same $T$. Then\n$X=M_2M_1^*$ commutes with every $W(z)$ by applying their two covariance\nidentities. Since the Weyl system is irreducible by [F1], every spectral\nprojection of either bounded self-adjoint operator\n$\\operatorname{Re}X=(X+X^*)/2$ and\n$\\operatorname{Im}X=(X-X^*)/(2i)$ commutes with every $W(z)$ and has invariant\nrange. Irreducibility forces each such projection to be $0$ or $I$. The\nspectral measure in [F4] then implies each self-adjoint part is scalar: if a\nbounded self-adjoint operator had two distinct spectral values, disjoint\nneighborhoods of those values would yield a nonzero proper spectral\nprojection. Hence $X=\\lambda I$ for some $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb C$; since $X$ is\nunitary, $|\\lambda|=1$. Thus $M_1,M_2$ differ only by a phase.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement assumes r>=1 and hbar>0."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 includes the zero Weyl label and identity symplectic map."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement and steps 1.1 and 2.1: the generator formulas include the r=1 case."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement and step 2.1: [F2] includes singular-block matrices through the factorization lemma."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Steps 1.1 and 2.1 include inverse and identity generators."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement and step 1.1: AC and its regular-irreducibility and spectral consequences are stated in the assumptions."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The result is existence and phase uniqueness, not an iff."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The result is existence and phase uniqueness, not an iff."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "case": "one-mode chirp",
          "calculation": "C_X*W(q,p)C_X=W(q+Xp,p)",
          "evidence": "step 1.1"
        },
        {
          "case": "Fourier covariance",
          "calculation": "F W(q,p)F*=W(p,-q)",
          "evidence": "step 2.1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lem-nrqmx-finite-maslov-phase-parity": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-finite-mode-symplectic-data",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "A density operator is a positive trace-class operator $\\rho$ with\n$\\operatorname{tr}\\rho=1$. Its quadrature second moments are finite when, for\none (equivalently every) spectral expansion\n$\\rho=\\sum_n w_n|\\psi_n\\rangle\\langle\\psi_n|$ with $w_n>0$,\n$\\sum_nw_n\\|R_j\\psi_n\\|^2<\\infty$ for every $j$. For such a state define\n$$d_j=\\sum_nw_n\\langle R_j\\psi_n,\\psi_n\\rangle,\\qquad\nV_{jk}=\\operatorname{Re}\\sum_nw_n\n\\langle(R_j-d_jI)\\psi_n,(R_k-d_kI)\\psi_n\\rangle.$$\nThese quantities are independent of the chosen orthonormal eigenbasis in\neach eigenspace. The state is Gaussian when its Weyl characteristic function\nhas the form\n$$\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho W(z))=\n\\exp\\!\\left(i z^Td-\\tfrac12z^TVz\\right)\\quad(z\\in\\mathbb R^{2r}),$$\nwith real $d$ and real symmetric $V$. Here $R_j$ has units\n$\\sqrt{\\mathrm{action}}$, each Weyl label has units\n$1/\\sqrt{\\mathrm{action}}$, and $V$ has units of action. No finite particle\nnumber truncation is used.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Let $\\mu_1,\\mu_2$ be the Maslov indices of the two paths. Their endpoint\nmatrix is the same $S$, so the upper-right block $B$, the determinant factor,\nand the homogeneous generating function $G_S$ in [F1] are the same. Dividing\nthe two kernel formulas shows that the propagators differ by the scalar\n$e^{i\\pi(\\mu_2-\\mu_1)/2}$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "By [F2], both $\\mu_j+r/2$ are integers and have the same parity because\nthey give the same $\\operatorname{sign}\\det B$. Thus $\\mu_2-\\mu_1$ is an even\ninteger. Consequently\n$$e^{i\\pi(\\mu_2-\\mu_1)/2}\\in\\{1,-1\\},$$\nwhich proves the claim in this generating chart. Multiplication by a fixed\nunitary does not change the relative scalar, so the final conditional claim\nfollows as well.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement assumes r>=1."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The generating chart requires det B!=0."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement and step 2.1: the parity calculation applies when r=1."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Singular B is excluded from this chart statement."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 compares paths with the same endpoint S."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No choice axiom is used in the finite path-index parity calculation."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The result is a phase-parity implication."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The result is a phase-parity implication."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "case": "same-endpoint phase ratio",
          "calculation": "An even Maslov-index difference gives ratio exp(i pi Delta mu/2) in {1,-1}",
          "evidence": "step 2.1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "thm-nrqmx-finite-metaplectic-construction": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-finite-mode-symplectic-data",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "By the finite-dimensional Stone–von Neumann theorem, every\nnonzero irreducible regular Weyl system with this finite phase space and this\nsame nonzero central parameter is unitarily equivalent to it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-stone-von-neumann-for-finite-dimensional-weyl-systems",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$$W(v)W(w)=e^{-i\\lambda\\omega(v,w)/2}W(v+w).$$\nThen $W$ is unitarily equivalent to the Schrödinger Weyl system on $L^2(\\mathbb R^n)$, so there is exactly one unitary-equivalence class of irreducible regular Weyl systems with the fixed parameter $\\lambda$. In a symplectic basis the model is\n$$\\big(W_\\lambda(q,p)f\\big)(x)=e^{i\\lambda p\\cdot(x-q/2)}f(x-q),$$\nfor $\\omega((q,p),(q',p'))=q\\cdot p'-p\\cdot q'$. The statement assumes finite-dimensional phase space, irreducibility, strong continuity, and a fixed nonzero central parameter. It makes no claim about algebraic modules, nonregular representations, $\\lambda=0$, or infinitely many modes.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-finite-weyl-uniqueness-interface",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "on $L^2(\\mathbb R^n)$. Consequently, there is exactly one unitary-equivalence class of nonzero irreducible regular Weyl systems with this fixed finite-dimensional phase space and parameter. No conclusion is asserted for infinite-dimensional phase spaces, nonregular systems, or zero central parameter.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-finite-symplectic-generator-factorization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $r\\ge1$, $J_0=\\begin{pmatrix}0&I_r\\\\-I_r&0\\end{pmatrix}$, and\n$$\\operatorname{Sp}(2r,\\mathbb R)=\\{S\\in M_{2r}(\\mathbb R):S^TJ_0S=J_0\\}.$$\nEvery $S\\in\\operatorname{Sp}(2r,\\mathbb R)$ is a finite product of matrices\nof the following three types and their inverses:\n$$U_X=\\begin{pmatrix}I&X\\\\0&I\\end{pmatrix},\\quad\nL_Y=\\begin{pmatrix}I&0\\\\Y&I\\end{pmatrix},\\quad\nD_A=\\begin{pmatrix}A&0\\\\0&A^{-T}\\end{pmatrix},$$\nwhere $X,Y$ are real symmetric and $A$ is real invertible. Partial Fourier\nrotations, which act by $(q_j,p_j)\\mapsto(-p_j,q_j)$ on any selected set of\ncoordinates and fix the others, are also products of these matrices.\nIn addition, for every $S$ there is a product $G$ of these generators such\nthat the upper-left block of $SG$ is invertible.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-finite-symplectic-implementers",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. On $L^2(\\mathbb R^r)$, for $r\\ge1$ and $\\hbar>0$,\ndefine the Weyl unitaries in grouped label order $(q,p)\\in\\mathbb R^r\\oplus\n\\mathbb R^r$ by\n$$[W(q,p)f](x)=e^{i\\sqrt\\hbar\\,q\\cdot(x+\\sqrt\\hbar p/2)}f(x+\\sqrt\\hbar p).$$\nFor $J_0=\\begin{pmatrix}0&I\\\\-I&0\\end{pmatrix}$, every\n$T\\in\\operatorname{Sp}(2r,\\mathbb R)$ has a unitary $M_T$ such that\n$$M_T^*W(z)M_T=W(Tz)\\quad(z\\in\\mathbb R^{2r}).$$\nAny two such unitaries differ by a scalar of modulus one. The covariance\nidentity holds on all of $L^2$; the corresponding unbounded quadrature\nidentities hold on the common transported Schwartz domain. This is an\nindividual existence and phase-uniqueness result; it does not identify the\nglobal topology or kernel of a continuously chosen implementer family.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-finite-maslov-phase-parity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $r\\ge1$, $\\hbar>0$, and let $S$ be a real finite-mode symplectic matrix\nin grouped order $(q,p)$ whose upper-right block $B$ is invertible. Consider\ntwo unitary propagators $U_1,U_2$ obtained from time-dependent real\nhomogeneous quadratic Hamiltonians (no linear or constant term), both with\nclassical endpoint $S$. Use the normalization that each propagator starts at\nthe identity and solves the Schrödinger equation with Planck scale $\\hbar$.\nThen\n$$U_2=U_1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad U_2=-U_1.$$\nThe same conclusion holds after multiplying both propagators by a fixed\nnormalized quadratic propagator, provided the resulting endpoint lies in a\nchart with invertible upper-right block.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-finite-mode-fock-schr-identification",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $g_N(x)=\\prod_{j=1}^r g_{n_j}(x_j)$ be the normalized product Hermite",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-nrqm-oscillator-multidimensional-oscillator-completeness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $H$ be the diagonal operator of [[lem-nrqm-oscillator-diagonal-adjoint-and-graph-core-lemma]] for the enumerated family $\\psi_{\\varphi(m)}$ and eigenvalue sequence $E_{\\varphi(m)}$, where $E_N:=\\hbar\\sum_{j=1}^d\\omega_j(n_j+\\frac12)$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Reorder interleaved coordinates by the fixed permutation\n$P(Q_1,P_1,\\ldots,Q_r,P_r)=(Q_1,\\ldots,Q_r,P_1,\\ldots,P_r)$, and write\n$W_g(w)=W(P^Tw)$ for grouped labels. Then $J_0=PJP^T$ and\n$S_g=PSP^T$ obeys $S_g^TJ_0S_g=J_0$. Apply [F3] directly to the grouped Weyl\nsystem on $L^2(\\mathbb R^r)$ with label matrix $S_g^T$; this matrix is\nsymplectic because $S_g^TJ_0S_g=J_0$ also implies $S_gJ_0S_g^T=J_0$.\nSince\n$S_g^TP=PS^T$, converting labels back gives\n$$M_S^*W(z)M_S=M_S^*W_g(Pz)M_S=W_g(S_g^TPz)=W(S^Tz).$$\nDifferentiating the bounded one-parameter Weyl identity on the common\nSchwartz core gives $M_S^*RM_S=SR$. For the rest of the proof use grouped\ncoordinates and write $S$ for $S_g$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Define $\\operatorname{Mp}(2r,\\mathbb R)$ to be the set of unitary\nSchwartz automorphisms obtained by normalized Schrödinger evolution along\npiecewise-smooth time-dependent real homogeneous quadratic Hamiltonians\n$$H_t=\\frac14\\sum_{j,k}K_{jk}(t)(R_jR_k+R_kR_j),\n\\qquad K(t)=K(t)^T\\in M_{2r}(\\mathbb R),$$\nwith no linear or scalar term. Concatenation of Hamiltonian paths gives\ncomposition of their unitaries by [F4]; reversing a path gives the inverse.\nOn Schwartz space, differentiating $U(t)^*RU(t)$ and using\n$[R_j,R_k]=i\\hbar J_{jk}$ gives the classical linear Hamilton equation\n$\\dot S=JK(t)S$. Thus each such propagator has a unique symplectic endpoint,\nand the endpoint map $\\pi$ is a group homomorphism. Uniqueness follows\nbecause the explicit Weyl formula has no nonzero label acting as a scalar:\nif its translation coordinate is nonzero it translates Schwartz functions,\nand if only its multiplication coordinate is nonzero it multiplies by a\nnonconstant exponential. The Weyl product law then makes the endpoint label\nmatrix unique.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Every generator from [F2] is a path endpoint of a homogeneous quadratic\nHamiltonian. A symmetric shear is joined to the identity by scaling its\nsymmetric block, and a partial Fourier rotation is joined by varying the\noscillator angle. If $\\det A>0$, polar\ndecomposition writes $A=OP$ with $O\\in SO(r)$ and $P$ positive definite.\nThe path $P_t=(1-t)I+tP$ stays positive definite, and an orthogonal matrix\nin $SO(r)$ is a product of plane rotations, each joined to the identity by\nvarying its angle. These paths connect the coordinate change to the\nidentity. If\n$\\det A<0$, factor one coordinate reflection from a positive-determinant\nchange; that reflection is a $\\pi$ oscillator rotation on that mode.\nThis gives a piecewise-smooth symplectic path for each generator. Along any\nsuch path $S_t$, the matrix $X_t=\\dot S_tS_t^{-1}$ satisfies\n$X_t^TJ_0+J_0X_t=0$; hence $K_t=-J_0X_t$ is symmetric and\n$\\dot S_t=J_0K_tS_t$. The symmetrically ordered quadratic with matrix $K_t$\ntherefore has this classical endpoint. Thus [F2] supplies, for every $S$, a\nproduct of normalized quadratic propagators whose endpoint is $S$, proving\n$\\pi$ is onto.\nThe one-parameter paths also show that each such product belongs to the\nidentity component of $\\operatorname{Mp}$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "If $M_1,M_2$ implement the same $S$, then [F3] makes\n$M_2M_1^*=\\lambda I$ for some $|\\lambda|=1$. For any other representation\n$W'$ in [F1], choose a unitary $V$ with $VW'(z)V^*=W(z)$ by the\nfinite-dimensional Stone–von Neumann theorem. Then\n$M'_S=V^*M_SV$ is unitary and\n$$M_S'^*W'(z)M'_S=V^*M_S^*W(z)M_SV=W'(S^Tz).$$\nConjugating the phase-uniqueness statement by $V$ proves the same result in\nthat representation.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "To bound a fiber, first suppose the grouped upper-right block $B$ of $S$\nis invertible. Any two normalized propagators over $S$ differ by sign by\n[F5]. For general $S$, the singular-block factorization in [F2] provides a\nright factor $G$ for which the upper-left block of $SG$ is invertible.\nRight-multiply by the quarter-turn\n$J_0=\\begin{psmallmatrix}0&I\\\\-I&0\\end{psmallmatrix}$; the upper-right block\nof $SGJ_0$ is that invertible upper-left block. If $M_1,M_2$ lie over $S$,\nthen $M_1M_{GJ_0}$ and $M_2M_{GJ_0}$ are normalized propagators over $SGJ_0$;\nthe chart case makes them differ only by $\\pm1$. Cancelling the common\nright factor proves every fiber of $\\pi$ has at most two elements.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "The identity has two distinct lifts. The constant zero Hamiltonian gives\n$I$. On the first mode (and as the identity on the other modes if $r>1$),\nthe homogeneous oscillator path\n$U(t)=e^{-itH/\\hbar}=e^{-it(N+1/2)}$ has classical endpoint the identity at\n$t=2\\pi$, because its Hamilton equations are $\\dot Q=P$, $\\dot P=-Q$ and\nmake one full rotation, while by [F6]\n$$U(2\\pi)|n\\rangle=e^{-2\\pi i(n+1/2)}|n\\rangle=-|n\\rangle$$\nfor every $n$. Completeness of the Hermite basis gives $U(2\\pi)=-I$. For any\n$S$, choose a normalized lift $\\widetilde M_S$ supplied by step 2.1. Then\n$\\widetilde M_S$ and $-\\widetilde M_S$ are two distinct lifts; step 3.1\nproves there are no others. The kernel is exactly $\\{I,-I\\}$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "It remains to verify the covering topology. For any endpoint $S_0$, choose\na fixed factor $T=GJ_0$ so $S_0T$ has invertible upper-right block; it stays\ninvertible on a neighborhood of $S_0$. For each $\\widetilde S=ST$ in this\nchart, $\\widetilde S J_0$ has invertible upper-left block, so the explicit\nfactorization in [F2] and its parameters in step 1.2 vary continuously with\nthe blocks. Shrink to a coordinate ball in this open chart and join its\ncenter to each endpoint by paths varying smoothly with the endpoint. The\ncorresponding $K_t=-J_0\\dot S_tS_t^{-1}$ varies continuously. The kernel in\n[F4] has continuously varying quadratic phase and determinant magnitude on\nthis chart. The endpoint-stratum parity [F5] leaves two possible signs; the\nchosen local paths select the sign continuously, which is locally constant\non the ball. The kernel, equivalently its chirp, coordinate-change and\nFourier factorization, therefore gives a continuous choice of normalized\nlift in the topology of $U$ and $U^{-1}$ on Schwartz space. Its negative is\na second continuous choice.\n\nThe endpoint map is continuous in this topology: its Weyl covariance is\ncontinuous on Schwartz functions, and the explicit Weyl formula recovers the\ntranslation coordinates from shifts and the remaining coordinates from the\nnonconstant multiplication phases. The two local choices are separated,\nsince on any fixed nonzero Schwartz function their values differ by twice\nthat vector. After shrinking the ball, their images are disjoint open sheets;\nthe continuous sections are inverse to $\\pi$ on each sheet. Multiplication\nby a fixed normalized lift of $T^{-1}$ transports these sheets to a\nneighborhood of $S_0$. The fiber bound in step 3.1 shows that they exhaust\neach fiber, so $\\pi$ is a two-fold covering map. Step 2.1 shows its total\ngroup is connected. This is the connected metaplectic double cover and its\ndefining unitary action is the metaplectic representation.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The theorem assumes r>=1 and hbar>0."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The zero Hamiltonian gives the identity lift in step 3.1."
        },
        {
