          "calculation": "A one-mode shear is implemented by a quadratic chirp",
          "evidence": "step 2.1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "thm-nrqmx-finite-mode-bogoliubov": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-creation-contraction-bounds",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a\ncomplex Hilbert space with inner product linear in the first argument\n([[def-hilbert-space]], [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]), with\ncompleted tensor powers $H^{\\otimes n}$, symmetric projections $P_+^{(n)}$,\nFock space $F(H)$ and finite-particle space $F_0$ as in\n[[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-hilbert-direct-sum-construction]] and\n[[lem-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-fock-sector-projections]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-finite-mode-fock-schr-identification",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then $(|N\\rangle)_N$ and $(g_N)_N$ are complete orthonormal families, and the\nmap $\\mathcal U|N\\rangle=g_N$ extends uniquely to a unitary\n$$\\mathcal U:\\mathcal F_s(\\mathbb C^r)\\longrightarrow L^2(\\mathbb R^r).$$\nOn the algebraic finite-particle span, $\\mathcal U a_j\\mathcal U^{-1}$ and\n$\\mathcal U a_j^*\\mathcal U^{-1}$ are respectively\n$(x_j+\\partial_{x_j})/\\sqrt2$ and $(x_j-\\partial_{x_j})/\\sqrt2$ on product\nHermite functions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-finite-metaplectic-construction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC, $r\\ge1$, and $\\hbar>0$. Let $R=(Q_1,P_1,\\ldots,Q_r,P_r)^T$,\n$J=\\bigoplus_{j=1}^r\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\-1&0\\end{pmatrix}$,\nand let $W(z)$ be the regular irreducible Weyl system of\n[[def-nrqmx-finite-mode-symplectic-data]]. For every real\n$S\\in\\operatorname{Sp}(2r,\\mathbb R)$, there is a unitary $M_S$ on\n$L^2(\\mathbb R^r)$ such that\n$$M_S^*W(z)M_S=W(S^Tz)\\qquad(z\\in\\mathbb R^{2r});$$\nequivalently, $M_S^*RM_S=SR$ on the common transported Schwartz core.\nAny two such implementers differ by a scalar of modulus one. The same\nconstruction applies in every nonzero irreducible regular representation\nwith this finite phase space and fixed central parameter $\\hbar$, after the\nunitary equivalence of\n[[thm-nrqmx-finite-weyl-uniqueness-interface]].",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-finite-mode-fock-schr-identification",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $r\\ge1$, let $H=\\mathbb C^r$ with orthonormal basis\n$e_1,\\ldots,e_r$, and let $\\mathcal F_s(H)=\\bigoplus_{n\\ge0}H^{\\otimes_s n}$\nbe the bosonic Fock space with vacuum $\\Omega$ and creation and annihilation\noperators $a_j^*,a_j$ from\n[[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-hilbert-direct-sum-construction]] and\n[[lem-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-creation-contraction-bounds]]. For\n$N=(n_1,\\ldots,n_r)\\in\\mathbb N_0^r$, set\n$$|N\\rangle:=\\prod_{j=1}^r\\frac{(a_j^*)^{n_j}}{\\sqrt{n_j!}}\\Omega.$$\nLet $g_N(x)=\\prod_{j=1}^r g_{n_j}(x_j)$ be the normalized product Hermite\nfunction from [[thm-nrqm-oscillator-multidimensional-oscillator-completeness]].\nThen $(|N\\rangle)_N$ and $(g_N)_N$ are complete orthonormal families, and the\nmap $\\mathcal U|N\\rangle=g_N$ extends uniquely to a unitary\n$$\\mathcal U:\\mathcal F_s(\\mathbb C^r)\\longrightarrow L^2(\\mathbb R^r).$$\nOn the algebraic finite-particle span, $\\mathcal U a_j\\mathcal U^{-1}$ and\n$\\mathcal U a_j^*\\mathcal U^{-1}$ are respectively\n$(x_j+\\partial_{x_j})/\\sqrt2$ and $(x_j-\\partial_{x_j})/\\sqrt2$ on product\nHermite functions. In particular the quadratures\n$$Q_j=\\sqrt{\\hbar/2}(a_j+a_j^*),\\qquad\nP_j=-i\\sqrt{\\hbar/2}(a_j-a_j^*)$$\nact there as multiplication by $\\sqrt\\hbar x_j$ and as\n$-i\\sqrt\\hbar\\,\\partial_{x_j}$, respectively. These are core identities;\nthey do not assert that a finite number cutoff preserves the canonical\ncommutation relations.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Expand the commutator using [F1]. The only nonzero terms in\n$[a'_j,(a'_k)^*]$ are\n$$\\sum_lU_{jl}\\overline{U_{kl}}-\\sum_lV_{jl}\\overline{V_{kl}}\n=(UU^*-VV^*)_{jk}=\\delta_{jk}.$$\nLikewise\n$$[a'_j,a'_k]=\\sum_l(U_{jl}V_{kl}-V_{jl}U_{kl})\n=(UV^T-VU^T)_{jk}=0.$$\nThese are finite sums on the common core, so no unbounded product is used\noutside its domain.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Substitute $a=(Q+iP)/\\sqrt{2\\hbar}$ and\n$a^*=(Q-iP)/\\sqrt{2\\hbar}$ into $Ua+Va^*$. Its real quadrature part is\n$\\operatorname{Re}(U+V)Q-\\operatorname{Im}(U-V)P$ and its imaginary part is\n$\\operatorname{Im}(U+V)Q+\\operatorname{Re}(U-V)P$. This is exactly $S_gR$.\nSince step 1.1 gives the canonical commutators for these transformed\nquadratures, bilinearity yields\n$$[ (S_gR)_j,(S_gR)_k]=i\\hbar(S_gJ_0S_g^T)_{jk}\n=i\\hbar(J_0)_{jk}.$$\nAs $\\hbar>0$, this matrix identity implies $S_gJ_0S_g^T=J_0$, hence\n$S_g$ is invertible and symplectic.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Let $P$ be the fixed permutation from interleaved to grouped coordinates\nand set $S_i=P^TS_gP$. The symplectic identity for $S_g$ makes $S_i$\nsymplectic in interleaved order. Apply [F3] to $S_i$ and transport its\nunitary to Fock by [F4]. Converting by $P$ gives\n$M^*W_g(w)M=W_g(S_g^Tw)$ on every label and $M^*R_gM=S_gR_g$ on the\ntransported Schwartz core. The Weyl covariance holds on all Fock space\nbecause each Weyl operator and the implementer are bounded. The complex\nrecombination in step 2.1 gives\n$M^*aM=a'$. Each generator in [F3] preserves Schwartz space, so $M\\Omega$\nlies in that core. Therefore, using $a_j\\Omega=0$ and\n$a'_j\\Omega=\\sum_kV_{jk}a_k^*\\Omega$, one has\n$$\\sum_j\\|a_jM\\Omega\\|^2\n=\\sum_j\\|M a'_j\\Omega\\|^2\n=\\sum_{j,k}|V_{jk}|^2=\\operatorname{tr}(VV^*).$$\nThe equality is finite and proves the claimed finite mean number. No step\npasses to infinitely many modes.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement assumes r>=1."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement and steps 1.1 and 3.1: U=I,V=0 gives the identity and zero transformed-vacuum number."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement and step 2.1: the finite matrix formulas specialize to r=1."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "No nonsingularity of V is assumed; step 1.1 uses the full stated CCR identities."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The vacuum is included in step 3.1."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement and step 3.1: AC is used through the finite symplectic implementer."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The theorem proves implementation from the matrix hypotheses."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The theorem proves implementation from the matrix hypotheses."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "case": "one-mode squeeze",
          "calculation": "U=cosh(s), V=sinh(s) gives mean number sinh^2(s)",
          "evidence": "step 3.1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lem-nrqmx-williamson-normal-form": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-finite-mode-symplectic-data",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice, an integer $r\\ge1$, and an action scale\n$\\hbar>0$. Inner products are linear in the first argument\n([[def-inner-product-space]]). Use the Fock-to-Schrödinger unitary\n$\\mathcal U:\\mathcal F_s(\\mathbb C^r)\\to L^2(\\mathbb R^r)$ of\n[[lem-nrqmx-finite-mode-fock-schr-identification]]. In Schrödinger coordinates\ndefine the self-adjoint quadratures\n$$Q_j=\\mathcal U^{-1}(\\sqrt\\hbar\\,x_j)\\mathcal U,\\qquad P_j=\\mathcal U^{-1}(-i\\sqrt\\hbar\\,\\partial_{x_j})\\mathcal U,$$\nwhere $x_j$ is the real multiplication operator on its maximal domain and\n$-i\\partial_{x_j}$ is defined by conjugating that multiplication operator by\nthe unitary Fourier transform. Their common Schwartz core satisfies\n$$[Q_j,Q_k]=[P_j,P_k]=0,\\qquad [Q_j,P_k]=i\\hbar\\delta_{jk}I.$$\nWrite $R=(Q_1,P_1,\\ldots,Q_r,P_r)^T$ and\n$J=\\bigoplus_{j=1}^r\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\-1&0\\end{pmatrix}$.\nThe Weyl unitary labelled by $z=(q_1,p_1,\\ldots,q_r,p_r)$ is the unitary\nwhose grouped-coordinate formula, with $q=(q_1,\\ldots,q_r)$ and\n$p=(p_1,\\ldots,p_r)$, is\n$$[W(z)f](x)=e^{i\\sqrt\\hbar\\,q\\cdot(x+\\sqrt\\hbar p/2)}f(x+\\sqrt\\hbar p).$$\nIt obeys $W(z)W(z')=e^{-i\\hbar z^TJz'/2}W(z+z')$ and is regular and\nirreducible. By the finite-dimensional Stone–von Neumann theorem, every\nnonzero irreducible regular Weyl system with this finite phase space and this\nsame nonzero central parameter is unitarily equivalent to it.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "First diagonalize $V$ by the Rayleigh-quotient induction in [F1]: a\nmaximizer of $x^TVx$ on the unit sphere is an eigenvector, because the\nderivative at zero of the quotient at $e+tv$ vanishes for every $v\\perp e$;\nthe orthogonal complement is invariant under a symmetric matrix, so induction\ngives an orthonormal eigenbasis. Positivity makes each eigenvalue positive,\nand replacing it by its positive square root constructs the real symmetric\npositive definite $M=V^{1/2}$. Now let $K=MJM$. Then $K^T=-K$, and $K$ is\ninvertible because both $M$ and $J$ are. The matrix $B=-K^2=K^TK$ is real\nsymmetric positive definite. Apply the same Rayleigh-quotient argument to $B$\nto choose a unit eigenvector $e$ with eigenvalue $\\nu^2>0$ and put\n$f=-Ke/\\nu$. Since\n$\\|Ke\\|^2=e^TBe=\\nu^2$, $f$ is unit; since $K$ is skew,\n$e^Tf=0$. Also $Ke=-\\nu f$ and\n$Kf=-K^2e/\\nu=\\nu e$. Thus in the orthonormal ordered pair $(e,f)$,\n$$\\begin{pmatrix}e^TKe&e^TKf\\\\f^TKe&f^TKf\\end{pmatrix}\n=\\begin{pmatrix}0&\\nu\\\\-\\nu&0\\end{pmatrix}.$$\nFor any $x$ orthogonal to both $e,f$, skew-symmetry gives\n$e^TKx=-(Ke)^Tx=\\nu f^Tx=0$ and\n$f^TKx=-(Kf)^Tx=-\\nu e^Tx=0$, so their orthogonal complement is\n$K$-invariant. Repeating the argument on this complement gives an\northogonal matrix $O$ and positive $\\nu_j$ with\n$O^TKO=JD$, where $D=\\operatorname{diag}(\\nu_1,\\nu_1,\\ldots,\\nu_r,\\nu_r)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Set $S=MO D^{-1/2}$. Then\n$$SDS^T=MO D^{-1/2}D D^{-1/2}O^TM=M^2=V,$$\nand\n$$S^TJS=D^{-1/2}O^TMJM O D^{-1/2}\n=D^{-1/2}(JD)D^{-1/2}=J,$$\nbecause $D$ commutes with $J$. This proves the Williamson factorization and\nsymplecticity of $S$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For symplectic $S$, congruence by $S^{-1}$ maps\n$V+i\\hbar J/2$ to $D+i\\hbar J/2$, since\n$S^{-1}JS^{-T}=J$. In each $(Q_j,P_j)$ block the latter matrix is\n$$\\begin{pmatrix}\\nu_j&i\\hbar/2\\\\-i\\hbar/2&\\nu_j\\end{pmatrix},$$\nwhose eigenvalues are $\\nu_j+\\hbar/2$ and $\\nu_j-\\hbar/2$. Congruence by an\ninvertible matrix preserves nonnegative quadratic forms, so the full matrix\nis positive semidefinite exactly when every $\\nu_j\\ge\\hbar/2$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement assumes r>=1."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "V is required to be positive definite."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
