    "thm-nrqmx-gaussian-covariance-uncertainty": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-composites-density-and-scalar-spectral-probability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "(b) Let $E$ be a projection-valued measure on a measurable space $(X,\\Sigma)$ acting on $H$ ([[def-projection-valued-measure]]). Then $\\mu_\\rho(B):=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho E(B))$ is a well-defined probability measure on $(X,\\Sigma)$ ([[def-measure]], [[def-probability-measure]]); it is given by the absolutely convergent series $\\mu_\\rho(B)=\\sum_nw_n\\|E(B)u_n\\|^2$ in every expansion of (a), and reduces to $\\mu_\\rho(B)=\\|E(B)u\\|^2$ for a pure density operator $\\rho=T_{u,u}$ with $\\|u\\|=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-finite-mode-symplectic-data",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "A density operator is a positive trace-class operator $\\rho$ with\n$\\operatorname{tr}\\rho=1$. Its quadrature second moments are finite when, for\none (equivalently every) spectral expansion\n$\\rho=\\sum_n w_n|\\psi_n\\rangle\\langle\\psi_n|$ with $w_n>0$,\n$\\sum_nw_n\\|R_j\\psi_n\\|^2<\\infty$ for every $j$. For such a state define\n$$d_j=\\sum_nw_n\\langle R_j\\psi_n,\\psi_n\\rangle,\\qquad\nV_{jk}=\\operatorname{Re}\\sum_nw_n\n\\langle(R_j-d_jI)\\psi_n,(R_k-d_kI)\\psi_n\\rangle.$$\nThese quantities are independent of the chosen orthonormal eigenbasis in\neach eigenspace. The state is Gaussian when its Weyl characteristic function\nhas the form\n$$\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho W(z))=\n\\exp\\!\\left(i z^Td-\\tfrac12z^TVz\\right)\\quad(z\\in\\mathbb R^{2r}),$$\nwith real $d$ and real symmetric $V$. Here $R_j$ has units\n$\\sqrt{\\mathrm{action}}$, each Weyl label has units\n$1/\\sqrt{\\mathrm{action}}$, and $V$ has units of action. No finite particle\nnumber truncation is used.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-finite-mode-symplectic-data",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "the unitary Fourier transform. Their common Schwartz core satisfies",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-finite-metaplectic-construction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "There is also a connected topological group\n$\\operatorname{Mp}(2r,\\mathbb R)$ of normalized propagators of real\nhomogeneous quadratic Hamiltonians on this finite-mode Schrödinger space.\nHere a unitary Schwartz automorphism means a unitary on $L^2(\\mathbb R^r)$\nwhose restriction is a continuous linear bijection of the Schwartz space\n$\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^r)$ with continuous inverse. A normalized propagator\nstarts at the identity and solves $i\\hbar\\,\\partial_tU(t)=H_tU(t)$ for a\nreal symmetrically ordered quadratic $H_t$ with no linear or scalar term.\nGive $\\operatorname{Mp}$ the topology of pointwise convergence of $U$ and\n$U^{-1}$ on $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^r)$ in its Schwartz topology; give\n$\\operatorname{Sp}(2r,\\mathbb R)$ its usual matrix topology.\nThe map\n$$\\pi:\\operatorname{Mp}(2r,\\mathbb R)\\longrightarrow\n\\operatorname{Sp}(2r,\\mathbb R),\\qquad M\\longmapsto S\n\\quad\\text{when }M^*W(z)M=W(S^Tz),$$\nis a two-fold covering homomorphism. Its two points over $S$ act by $M_S$\nand $-M_S$ when $M_S$ denotes either normalized lift; an arbitrary Weyl\nimplementer may differ from them by any scalar phase. This connected double\ncover and its unitary action are the metaplectic group and metaplectic\nrepresentation. All mode counts here are finite; no infinite-mode\nimplementation claim is part of the statement.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-finite-mode-bogoliubov",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "There is a unitary $M$ on finite-mode Fock, obtained by transporting an\nindividual finite symplectic implementer through the Fock–Schrödinger\nunitary, such that $M^*aM=a'$ on the transported Schwartz core and\n$M^*W(z)M=W(S_g^Tz)$ in grouped Weyl-label order.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-williamson-normal-form",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $r\\ge1$, $\\hbar>0$, and\n$J=\\bigoplus_{j=1}^r\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\-1&0\\end{pmatrix}$.\nFor every real symmetric positive definite $2r\\times2r$ matrix $V$, there\nare an interleaved symplectic matrix $S$ ($S^TJS=J$) and positive numbers\n$\\nu_1,\\ldots,\\nu_r$ such that\n$$V=S D S^T,\\qquad D=\\operatorname{diag}(\\nu_1,\\nu_1,\\ldots,\\nu_r,\\nu_r).$$\nMoreover,\n$$V+i\\hbar J/2\\succeq0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\nu_j\\ge\\hbar/2\\ \\text{for every }j.$$\nHere $A\\succeq0$ means the Hermitian matrix $A$ has nonnegative quadratic\nform on every complex vector.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be an arbitrary measure space. All functions below have domain $X$. Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand hence\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-stone-one-parameter-unitary-groups",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. The map $T\\mapsto U(t)=e^{itT}$, computed by the\nBorel functional calculus of $T$\n([[thm-unbounded-borel-functional-calculus]]), is a bijection from the set of\nself-adjoint operators on $H$ onto the set of strongly continuous\none-parameter unitary groups on $H$. Its inverse assigns to $U$ its generator\n$G$ and the self-adjoint operator $T=-iG$; here $D(T)=D(G)$ is exactly the set\nof vectors at which $t\\mapsto U(t)x$ is differentiable at $0$, and\n$\\frac1t(U(t)x-x)\\to iTx$ for $x\\in D(T)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-finite-mode-fock-schr-identification",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "be the bosonic Fock space with vacuum $\\Omega$ and creation and annihilation",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-creation-contraction-bounds",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a\ncomplex Hilbert space with inner product linear in the first argument\n([[def-hilbert-space]], [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]), with\ncompleted tensor powers $H^{\\otimes n}$, symmetric projections $P_+^{(n)}$,\nFock space $F(H)$ and finite-particle space $F_0$ as in\n[[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-hilbert-direct-sum-construction]] and\n[[lem-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-fock-sector-projections]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-finite-mode-symplectic-data",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$$Q_j=\\mathcal U^{-1}(\\sqrt\\hbar\\,x_j)\\mathcal U,\\qquad\nP_j=\\mathcal U^{-1}(-i\\sqrt\\hbar\\,\\partial_{x_j})\\mathcal U,$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-finite-mode-symplectic-data",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Its quadrature second moments are finite when, for\none (equivalently every) spectral expansion\n$\\rho=\\sum_n w_n|\\psi_n\\rangle\\langle\\psi_n|$ with $w_n>0$,\n$\\sum_nw_n\\|R_j\\psi_n\\|^2<\\infty$ for every $j$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-schwartz-joint-quadrature-graph-core",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice, let $r\\ge1$ and $\\hbar>0$, and use the\nSchrödinger quadratures of [[def-nrqmx-finite-mode-symplectic-data]]. Put\n$$D=\\{f\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^r):x_jf\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^r),\\ \\partial_jf\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^r)\\text{ for every }j\\}.$$\nFor\n$$\\|f\\|_{\\mathrm{quad}}:=\\|f\\|_2+\\sum_{j=1}^r(\\|x_jf\\|_2+\\|\\partial_jf\\|_2),$$\nthe Schwartz space $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^r)$ is dense in $D$. The weak\nderivatives in this definition are taken in the distributional sense and\nagree with the Fourier-defined momentum\ndomains in the cited quadrature definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Write $X_j=R_j-d_jI$ and\n$G_{jk}=\\sum_nw_n\\langle X_j\\psi_n,X_k\\psi_n\\rangle$. The series converge\nabsolutely by Cauchy–Schwarz and the second-moment bounds. On compactly\nsupported smooth functions, integration by parts gives\n$$\\langle R_jf,R_kf\\rangle-\\langle R_kf,R_jf\\rangle\n=-i\\hbar J_{jk}\\|f\\|^2.$$\nFor the same-mode $Q_j,P_j$ pair, this is\n$i\\hbar\\int x_j\\partial_j|f|^2=-i\\hbar\\|f\\|^2$ after the vanishing\nboundary term; distinct coordinate pairs and equal-type pairs give zero.\nThe joint graph-norm density in [F2] extends the identity to each\n$\\psi_n$ in the common domain. Centering by the real scalars $d_j$ does not\nchange the antisymmetric difference. Summing with weights yields\n$G-G^T=-i\\hbar J$, while $\\operatorname{Re}G=V$ by definition; hence\n$G=V-i\\hbar J/2$. For any $c\\in\\mathbb C^{2r}$,\n$$\\sum_nw_n\\left\\|\\sum_jc_jX_j\\psi_n\\right\\|^2\n=\\overline c^{\\,T}G^Tc\\ge0.$$\nSince $G^T=V+i\\hbar J/2$, the uncertainty matrix is positive\nsemidefinite.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Now suppose the target $V+i\\hbar J/2$ is positive semidefinite. For real\n$x$, $x^TVx=x^*(V+i\\hbar J/2)x\\ge0$, so $V$ is nonnegative. If a nonzero\nreal $x$ had $x^TVx=0$, put $H=V+i\\hbar J/2$. For every complex $y$ and\nreal $t$, positivity gives\n$(x+ty)^*H(x+ty)=2t\\operatorname{Re}(y^*Hx)+t^2y^*Hy\\ge0$. Allowing both\nsigns of $t$ forces $\\operatorname{Re}(y^*Hx)=0$; replacing $y$ by $iy$\nforces the imaginary part to vanish as well. This holds for every $y$, so\ntake $y=Hx$ to obtain $\\|Hx\\|^2=0$ and hence $Hx=0$. Its real and imaginary\nparts give $Vx=0$ and\n$Jx=0$, contradicting invertibility of $J$. Thus $V$ is positive definite.\nApply [F5] to obtain $V=SD S^T$ with\n$D=\\operatorname{diag}(\\nu_1,\\nu_1,\\ldots,\\nu_r,\\nu_r)$ and\n$\\nu_j\\ge\\hbar/2$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "For one mode and $\\bar n\\ge0$, set\n$$\\eta=\\frac{\\bar n}{1+\\bar n},\\qquad\n\\rho_{\\bar n}=(1-\\eta)\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty \\eta^m|m\\rangle\\langle m|,$$\nwith $\\rho_0=|0\\rangle\\langle0|$. The geometric series makes this a\npositive trace-class operator of trace one. For $\\bar n>0$, define\n$$|\\alpha\\rangle=e^{- |\\alpha|^2/2}\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty\n\\frac{\\alpha^m}{\\sqrt{m!}}|m\\rangle.$$\nIts norm is one by the exponential series. The vector\n$\\psi_\\alpha:=W(q_0,p_0)g_0$, with\n$\\alpha=\\sqrt{\\hbar/2}(-p_0+iq_0)$, is a scalar phase times $|\\alpha\\rangle$:\nthe differential formula gives $a\\psi=\\alpha\\psi$, and comparing\ncoefficients in the complete number basis gives the displayed series up to\none constant phase. In Schrödinger coordinates\n$$\\psi_\\alpha(x)=\\pi^{-1/4}\ne^{i\\sqrt\\hbar q_0(x+\\sqrt\\hbar p_0/2)}\ne^{-(x+\\sqrt\\hbar p_0)^2/2}.$$\nSet $y=x+\\sqrt\\hbar p_0+\\sqrt\\hbar p/2$. In\n$\\langle W(q,p)\\psi_\\alpha,\\psi_\\alpha\\rangle$, the Gaussian factor is\n$e^{-y^2-\\hbar p^2/4}$ and the phase is\n$\\sqrt\\hbar qy-\\hbar q p_0+\\hbar q_0p$. The Gaussian integral\n$$\\pi^{-1/2}\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{-y^2+i\\sqrt\\hbar qy}\\,dy\n=e^{-\\hbar q^2/4}$$\ntherefore gives, for $u=\\operatorname{Re}\\alpha$ and $v=\\operatorname{Im}\\alpha$,\n$$\\langle W(q,p)\\psi_\\alpha,\\psi_\\alpha\\rangle\n=\\exp\\!\\left(i\\sqrt{2\\hbar}(qu+pv)\n-\\frac{\\hbar}{4}(q^2+p^2)\\right).$$\nThe phase is the mean $d=(\\sqrt{2\\hbar}u,\\sqrt{2\\hbar}v)$. The geometric\nweights have mean occupation\n$\\sum_m m(1-\\eta)\\eta^m=\\eta/(1-\\eta)=\\bar n$. For a number vector,\n$$Q|m\\rangle=\\sqrt{\\hbar/2}\\big(\\sqrt{m+1}|m+1\\rangle+\\sqrt m|m-1\\rangle\\big),$$\nand $P|m\\rangle=-i\\sqrt{\\hbar/2}(\\sqrt m|m-1\\rangle-\\sqrt{m+1}|m+1\\rangle)$\nby [F8]. Orthogonality of number vectors gives zero means and\n$\\langle m,Q^2m\\rangle=\\langle m,P^2m\\rangle=\\hbar(m+1/2)$. Also\n$QP+PQ=-i\\hbar(a^2-(a^*)^2)$, whose diagonal matrix entries vanish because\n$a^2$ and $(a^*)^2$ change number by two. Thus the diagonal state has zero\nsymmetrized cross covariance. Summing against the geometric weights gives\n$$\\langle Q^2\\rangle=\\langle P^2\\rangle\n=\\frac{\\hbar}{2}(2\\bar n+1)=\\nu.$$\nThus its quadrature second moments are finite and its covariance is $\\nu I_2$.\nThe probability density $(\\pi\\bar n)^{-1}e^{-|\\alpha|^2/\\bar n}$ on the\ncomplex plane has independent real and imaginary parts of variance\n$\\bar n/2$. Angular integration of\n$|\\alpha\\rangle\\langle\\alpha|$ against this density kills every off-diagonal\nnumber-basis matrix entry. For the $m$th diagonal entry, writing\n$x=|\\alpha|^2$ gives\n$$\\frac1{\\bar n m!}\\int_0^\\infty\ne^{-x(1+1/\\bar n)}x^m\\,dx\n=\\frac{\\bar n^m}{(1+\\bar n)^{m+1}}=(1-\\eta)\\eta^m,$$\nwhere the integral follows from $m$ integrations by parts and the angular\nintegral vanishes for unequal indices. Let\n$P_K=\\sum_{m=0}^K|m\\rangle\\langle m|$ and integrate the finite-rank operators\n$P_K|\\alpha\\rangle\\langle\\alpha|P_K$. Their diagonal entries just computed\ngive $\\sum_{m=0}^K(1-\\eta)\\eta^m|m\\rangle\\langle m|$, which converges in trace norm\nto $\\rho_{\\bar n}$. Also $P_K|\\alpha\\rangle\\to|\\alpha\\rangle$ in norm for\neach $\\alpha$, and\n$|\\langle W(q,p)P_K\\alpha,P_K\\alpha\\rangle|\\le1$. Dominated convergence\n[F6] therefore passes the characteristic function through the mixture. The\nindependent real and imaginary Gaussians, each of variance $\\bar n/2$, have\ncharacteristic factor $e^{-\\hbar\\bar n(q^2+p^2)/2}$ by completing the square.\nMultiplying it by the vacuum factor in the coherent formula gives\n$$\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho_{\\bar n}W(q,p))\n=e^{-\\hbar(2\\bar n+1)(q^2+p^2)/4}\n=e^{-\\nu(q^2+p^2)/2},\\qquad\n\\nu=\\hbar(\\bar n+1/2).$$\nFor $\\bar n=0$, the same formula and covariance follow from the direct\nvacuum Gaussian integral.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "F8",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Let $M_S$ be an implementer from [F3] and set $\\rho_S=M_S\\rho M_S^*$.\nThe Weyl identity and cyclicity of trace against a bounded operator give\n$$\\chi_{\\rho_S}(z)=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho M_S^*W(z)M_S)\n=\\chi_\\rho(S^Tz).$$\nFor a coordinate vector $e_j$, the explicit Weyl formula gives\n$W(te_j)=e^{itR_j}$. Therefore\n$M_S^*e^{itR_j}M_S=W(tS^Te_j)$; its derivative on Schwartz functions at zero\nis $i\\sum_kS_{jk}R_k$. By [F7], the two strongly continuous groups have\nunique self-adjoint generators, so\n$M_S^*R_jM_S=\\overline{\\sum_kS_{jk}R_k}$. The left side has Schwartz as a\ncore because every generator unitary in [F3] preserves Schwartz. By [F2],\nSchwartz is dense in the joint quadrature graph norm, so every vector with all\nquadrature second moments lies in the domain of these finite sums and the\nidentity acts there as written. Applying it to the eigenvectors of $\\rho$\nand using the absolutely convergent form sums in [F1] gives $d_S=Sd$ and\n$V_S=SVS^T$. For transformations arising from finite Bogoliubov matrices,\nthese are exactly the core and Weyl interfaces of [F4].",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F7",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Put $\\bar n_j=\\nu_j/\\hbar-1/2\\ge0$ and take the finite tensor product\nof the one-mode states $\\rho_{\\bar n_j}$. In the occupation basis it is\npositive trace class with trace one, and its characteristic function is the\nproduct of the one-mode functions from step 1.3, namely\n$\\exp(-z^TDz/2)$. Conjugate this product state by an implementer $M_S$ from\n[F3]; step 2.1 gives characteristic function\n$\\exp(-z^TSDS^Tz/2)=\\exp(-z^TVz/2)$. Finally set\n$z_0=Jd/\\hbar$ and conjugate by the Weyl unitary $W(z_0)$. The Weyl\ncommutation relation yields\n$$W(z_0)^*W(z)W(z_0)\n=e^{i\\hbar z_0^TJz}W(z)=e^{iz^Td}W(z),$$\nbecause $d=-\\hbar Jz_0$. The resulting unitary conjugate of the product\nthermal state is positive, trace class and trace one, and has exactly the\ntarget characteristic function. This proves sufficiency and completes the\nfinite-mode equivalence. No infinite-mode inference is used.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
