    "ex-nrqmx-single-mode-squeezing": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-finite-mode-fock-schr-identification",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $r\\ge1$, let $H=\\mathbb C^r$ with orthonormal basis\n$e_1,\\ldots,e_r$, and let $\\mathcal F_s(H)=\\bigoplus_{n\\ge0}H^{\\otimes_s n}$\nbe the bosonic Fock space with vacuum $\\Omega$ and creation and annihilation\noperators $a_j^*,a_j$ from\n[[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-hilbert-direct-sum-construction]] and\n[[lem-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-creation-contraction-bounds]]. For\n$N=(n_1,\\ldots,n_r)\\in\\mathbb N_0^r$, set\n$$|N\\rangle:=\\prod_{j=1}^r\\frac{(a_j^*)^{n_j}}{\\sqrt{n_j!}}\\Omega.$$\nLet $g_N(x)=\\prod_{j=1}^r g_{n_j}(x_j)$ be the normalized product Hermite\nfunction from [[thm-nrqm-oscillator-multidimensional-oscillator-completeness]].\nThen $(|N\\rangle)_N$ and $(g_N)_N$ are complete orthonormal families, and the\nmap $\\mathcal U|N\\rangle=g_N$ extends uniquely to a unitary\n$$\\mathcal U:\\mathcal F_s(\\mathbb C^r)\\longrightarrow L^2(\\mathbb R^r).$$\nOn the algebraic finite-particle span, $\\mathcal U a_j\\mathcal U^{-1}$ and\n$\\mathcal U a_j^*\\mathcal U^{-1}$ are respectively\n$(x_j+\\partial_{x_j})/\\sqrt2$ and $(x_j-\\partial_{x_j})/\\sqrt2$ on product\nHermite functions. In particular the quadratures\n$$Q_j=\\sqrt{\\hbar/2}(a_j+a_j^*),\\qquad\nP_j=-i\\sqrt{\\hbar/2}(a_j-a_j^*)$$\nact there as multiplication by $\\sqrt\\hbar x_j$ and as\n$-i\\sqrt\\hbar\\,\\partial_{x_j}$, respectively. These are core identities;\nthey do not assert that a finite number cutoff preserves the canonical\ncommutation relations.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-finite-mode-symplectic-data",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$$Q_j=\\mathcal U^{-1}(\\sqrt\\hbar\\,x_j)\\mathcal U,\\qquad\nP_j=\\mathcal U^{-1}(-i\\sqrt\\hbar\\,\\partial_{x_j})\\mathcal U,$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-creation-contraction-bounds",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "(a) *Contraction map.* For every $f\\in H$ and $n\\ge0$ there is a unique bounded\nlinear map $\\Psi_f^{(n)}:H^{\\otimes(n+1)}\\to H^{\\otimes n}$ with\n$$\\langle\\varphi,\\Psi_f^{(n)}\\chi\\rangle=\\langle f\\otimes\\varphi,\\chi\\rangle \\qquad(\\varphi\\in H^{\\otimes n},\\ \\chi\\in H^{\\otimes(n+1)})$$\n([[thm-riesz-representation-for-hilbert-space]]); it satisfies\n$\\|\\Psi_f^{(n)}\\|\\le\\|f\\|$,\n$\\Psi_f^{(n)}(u_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes u_{n+1})=\\langle u_1,f\\rangle\\,u_2\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes u_{n+1}$\non elementary tensors, $\\Psi_f^{(n)}(\\operatorname{ran}P_+^{(n+1)})\\subseteq\\operatorname{ran}P_+^{(n)}$,\nand the map $f\\mapsto\\Psi_f^{(n)}$ is conjugate-linear.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-finite-mode-fock-schr-identification",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $r\\ge1$, let $H=\\mathbb C^r$ with orthonormal basis\n$e_1,\\ldots,e_r$, and let $\\mathcal F_s(H)=\\bigoplus_{n\\ge0}H^{\\otimes_s n}$\nbe the bosonic Fock space with vacuum $\\Omega$ and creation and annihilation\noperators $a_j^*,a_j$ from\n[[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-hilbert-direct-sum-construction]] and\n[[lem-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-creation-contraction-bounds]]. For\n$N=(n_1,\\ldots,n_r)\\in\\mathbb N_0^r$, set\n$$|N\\rangle:=\\prod_{j=1}^r\\frac{(a_j^*)^{n_j}}{\\sqrt{n_j!}}\\Omega.$$\nLet $g_N(x)=\\prod_{j=1}^r g_{n_j}(x_j)$ be the normalized product Hermite\nfunction from [[thm-nrqm-oscillator-multidimensional-oscillator-completeness]].\nThen $(|N\\rangle)_N$ and $(g_N)_N$ are complete orthonormal families, and the\nmap $\\mathcal U|N\\rangle=g_N$ extends uniquely to a unitary\n$$\\mathcal U:\\mathcal F_s(\\mathbb C^r)\\longrightarrow L^2(\\mathbb R^r).$$\nOn the algebraic finite-particle span, $\\mathcal U a_j\\mathcal U^{-1}$ and\n$\\mathcal U a_j^*\\mathcal U^{-1}$ are respectively\n$(x_j+\\partial_{x_j})/\\sqrt2$ and $(x_j-\\partial_{x_j})/\\sqrt2$ on product\nHermite functions. In particular the quadratures\n$$Q_j=\\sqrt{\\hbar/2}(a_j+a_j^*),\\qquad\nP_j=-i\\sqrt{\\hbar/2}(a_j-a_j^*)$$\nact there as multiplication by $\\sqrt\\hbar x_j$ and as\n$-i\\sqrt\\hbar\\,\\partial_{x_j}$, respectively. These are core identities;\nthey do not assert that a finite number cutoff preserves the canonical\ncommutation relations.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "A change of variables shows $U_s$ is unitary, since\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R}|e^{-s/2}f(e^{-s}x)|^2dx\n=\\int_{\\mathbb R}|f(y)|^2dy.$$\nFor Schwartz $f$, direct substitution and the chain rule give\n$$U_s^*QU_sf=e^sQf,\\qquad U_s^*PU_sf=e^{-s}Pf.$$\nUsing $a=(Q+iP)/\\sqrt{2\\hbar}$ and\n$a^*=(Q-iP)/\\sqrt{2\\hbar}$, this becomes\n$$U_s^*aU_s=\\frac{e^s+e^{-s}}2a+\n\\frac{e^s-e^{-s}}2a^*=\\cosh(s)a+\\sinh(s)a^*.$$\nThe dilation maps Schwartz space onto itself, so all identities hold on the\nstated common core and the associated Weyl covariance extends everywhere by\nunitarity.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The ground function $g_0(x)=\\pi^{-1/4}e^{-x^2/2}$ is even, so its\nposition mean is zero; its derivative is odd, so its momentum mean is zero.\nThe Gaussian integral gives $\\int x^2|g_0(x)|^2dx=1/2$, and\n$g_0'=-xg_0$ gives both vacuum variances $\\hbar/2$. Moreover\n$\\langle Q\\Omega,P\\Omega\\rangle=-i\\hbar/2$, so the real symmetrized cross\ncovariance is zero. For $\\psi_s=U_s\\Omega$, the unitary conjugation formulas\nin step 1.1 give\n$$\\langle Q\\rangle_{\\psi_s}=e^s\\langle Q\\rangle_\\Omega=0,\n\\quad \\langle Q^2\\rangle_{\\psi_s}=e^{2s}\\hbar/2,$$\nand likewise $\\langle P\\rangle_{\\psi_s}=0$ and\n$\\langle P^2\\rangle_{\\psi_s}=e^{-2s}\\hbar/2$. The cross form is unchanged\nbecause the two scale factors multiply to one, so it remains zero. Therefore\nthe covariance determinant is the product of the two variances,\n$\\hbar^2/4$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Since $U_s$ maps Schwartz space to itself, $\\psi_s$ is in the domain of\nthe number form. By [F2] and step 2.1,\n$$\\langle a^*a\\rangle_{\\psi_s}\n=\\frac{\\langle Q^2+P^2\\rangle_{\\psi_s}}{2\\hbar}-\\frac12\n=\\frac{e^{2s}+e^{-2s}-2}{4}=\\sinh^2(s).$$\nThis is the model's finite one-mode number expectation.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The example is for exactly one mode."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement and steps 1.1, 2.1 and 3.1: at s=0 the formulas give vacuum number zero and variances hbar/2."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The entire example is a one-mode calculation."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "There is no mode multiplicity parameter."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement and step 2.1: the formulas hold for every real s and changing its sign swaps variances."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The explicit dilation computation introduces no choice axiom."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
