          "evidence": "statement"
        }
      ]
    },
    "cex-nrqmx-infinite-mode-vacuum-warning": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-finite-mode-bogoliubov",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "There is a unitary $M$ on finite-mode Fock, obtained by transporting an\nindividual finite symplectic implementer through the Fock–Schrödinger\nunitary, such that $M^*aM=a'$ on the transported Schwartz core and\n$M^*W(z)M=W(S_g^Tz)$ in grouped Weyl-label order.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-finite-mode-bogoliubov",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC, $r\\ge1$, and the finite-mode Fock conventions of\n[[def-nrqmx-finite-mode-symplectic-data]]. Let $U,V\\in M_r(\\mathbb C)$ obey\n$$UU^*-VV^*=I_r,\\qquad UV^T=VU^T,$$\nwhere $^*$ is conjugate transpose and $^T$ is transpose without conjugation.\nOn the finite Hermite core define\n$$a'_j=\\sum_k(U_{jk}a_k+V_{jk}a_k^*).$$\nThen the $a'_j$ satisfy $[a'_j,(a'_k)^*]=\\delta_{jk}I$ and\n$[a'_j,a'_k]=0$ on that core. The real matrix\n$$S_g=\\begin{pmatrix}\\operatorname{Re}(U+V)&-\\operatorname{Im}(U-V)\\\\\n\\operatorname{Im}(U+V)&\\operatorname{Re}(U-V)\\end{pmatrix}$$\nis symplectic in grouped coordinate order $(Q_1,\\ldots,Q_r,P_1,\\ldots,P_r)$.\nThere is a unitary $M$ on finite-mode Fock, obtained by transporting an\nindividual finite symplectic implementer through the Fock–Schrödinger\nunitary, such that $M^*aM=a'$ on the transported Schwartz core and\n$M^*W(z)M=W(S_g^Tz)$ in grouped Weyl-label order. The transformed vacuum\n$M\\Omega$ has finite mean number\n$$\\sum_j\\|a_jM\\Omega\\|^2=\\operatorname{tr}(VV^*).$$\nNo infinite-mode implementability assertion is made.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-finite-mode-symplectic-data",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "[[lem-nrqmx-finite-mode-fock-schr-identification]]. In Schrödinger coordinates",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-finite-mode-fock-schr-identification",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $r\\ge1$, let $H=\\mathbb C^r$ with orthonormal basis\n$e_1,\\ldots,e_r$, and let $\\mathcal F_s(H)=\\bigoplus_{n\\ge0}H^{\\otimes_s n}$\nbe the bosonic Fock space with vacuum $\\Omega$ and creation and annihilation\noperators $a_j^*,a_j$ from\n[[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-hilbert-direct-sum-construction]] and\n[[lem-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-creation-contraction-bounds]]. For\n$N=(n_1,\\ldots,n_r)\\in\\mathbb N_0^r$, set\n$$|N\\rangle:=\\prod_{j=1}^r\\frac{(a_j^*)^{n_j}}{\\sqrt{n_j!}}\\Omega.$$\nLet $g_N(x)=\\prod_{j=1}^r g_{n_j}(x_j)$ be the normalized product Hermite\nfunction from [[thm-nrqm-oscillator-multidimensional-oscillator-completeness]].\nThen $(|N\\rangle)_N$ and $(g_N)_N$ are complete orthonormal families, and the\nmap $\\mathcal U|N\\rangle=g_N$ extends uniquely to a unitary\n$$\\mathcal U:\\mathcal F_s(\\mathbb C^r)\\longrightarrow L^2(\\mathbb R^r).$$\nOn the algebraic finite-particle span, $\\mathcal U a_j\\mathcal U^{-1}$ and\n$\\mathcal U a_j^*\\mathcal U^{-1}$ are respectively\n$(x_j+\\partial_{x_j})/\\sqrt2$ and $(x_j-\\partial_{x_j})/\\sqrt2$ on product\nHermite functions. In particular the quadratures\n$$Q_j=\\sqrt{\\hbar/2}(a_j+a_j^*),\\qquad\nP_j=-i\\sqrt{\\hbar/2}(a_j-a_j^*)$$\nact there as multiplication by $\\sqrt\\hbar x_j$ and as\n$-i\\sqrt\\hbar\\,\\partial_{x_j}$, respectively. These are core identities;\nthey do not assert that a finite number cutoff preserves the canonical\ncommutation relations.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "A change of variables gives\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R}|U_sf(x)|^2dx\n=\\int_{\\mathbb R}|f(y)|^2dy,$$\nso $U_s$ is unitary. The chain rule for $Q=\\sqrt\\hbar x$ and\n$P=-i\\sqrt\\hbar\\partial_x$ gives\n$U_s^*aU_s=\\cosh(s)a+\\sinh(s)a^*$. The scalar pair obeys\n$|u|^2-|v|^2=1$ and $uv=vu$. For the $N$-mode product take\n$U_N=uI_N$ and $V_N=vI_N$; the finite Bogoliubov result [F1] supplies an\nimplementer for these coefficients. Write\n$\\psi_s(x)=U_sg_0(x)=e^{-s/2}\\pi^{-1/4}e^{-e^{-2s}x^2/2}$. Then\n$$a\\psi_s=\\frac{1-e^{-2s}}{\\sqrt2}x\\psi_s,\\qquad\n\\|a\\psi_s\\|^2=\\frac{(1-e^{-2s})^2}{2}\\frac{e^{2s}}2=\\sinh^2(s),$$\nbecause the probability density $|\\psi_s|^2$ has second position moment\n$e^{2s}/2$. By [F3], the $N$-mode Fock–Schrödinger unitary sends its vacuum\n$\\Omega_N$ to $g_0^{\\otimes N}$. On $L^2(\\mathbb R^N)$ define\n$$[U_s^{(N)}f](x_1,\\ldots,x_N)=e^{-Ns/2}\nf(e^{-s}x_1,\\ldots,e^{-s}x_N).$$\nChanging variables in all $N$ coordinates proves this is unitary. It is the\nproduct of the one-mode dilations, and the coordinatewise chain rule gives\n$U_s^{(N)*}a_jU_s^{(N)}=u a_j+v a_j^*$ for each $j$. The finite Bogoliubov\nstatement [F1] also supplies an implementer for this coefficient pair. Put\n$\\Psi_N=(U_s\\Omega)^{\\otimes N}$ under the same identification; its $j$th\nnumber expectation is the one-mode value $\\|a\\psi_s\\|^2=\\sinh^2(s)$ already\ncomputed above. The number operator is the sum of the $N$ mode number\noperators, so linearity gives\n$$\\langle\\Psi_N,\\mathcal N_N\\Psi_N\\rangle=N\\sinh^2(s).$$\nThe product unitary exists because it is the tensor product of finitely many\ncopies of this unitary. The same coefficient pair is covered by the finite\nBogoliubov statement [F2]; no infinite product is taken.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "In Schrödinger coordinates, $\\Omega=g_0$ and\n$U_sg_0(x)=e^{-s/2}\\pi^{-1/4}e^{-e^{-2s}x^2/2}$. Direct Gaussian integration\ngives\n$$\\langle g_0,U_sg_0\\rangle\n=\\pi^{-1/2}e^{-s/2}\\int_{\\mathbb R}\ne^{-(1+e^{-2s})x^2/2}\\,dx\n=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{e^s+e^{-s}}}=\\frac1{\\sqrt{\\cosh s}}.$$\nSince $U_s$ and $g_0$ are real and positive here, this is also the overlap\nmagnitude; the formula holds for every real $s$. Product inner products\nfactor over the $N$ modes, yielding\n$$|\\langle\\Omega_N,\\Psi_N\\rangle|=(\\cosh s)^{-N/2}.$$\nFor $s\\ne0$, $\\cosh s>1$, so this magnitude tends to zero as $N\\to\\infty$.\nTogether with step 1.1 this proves only the stated finite-$N$ divergence and\noverlap limit. Finite-dimensional implementation does not itself provide an\ninfinite-mode implementer, and this example does not prove that none exists.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The example assumes N>=1 and s!=0."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "At s=0 the formulas give zero number and unit overlap; the statement excludes this trivial case."
        },
