        }
      ]
    },
    "lem-nrqmx-schwartz-joint-quadrature-graph-core": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-finite-mode-symplectic-data",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice, an integer $r\\ge1$, and an action scale\n$\\hbar>0$. Inner products are linear in the first argument\n([[def-inner-product-space]]). Use the Fock-to-Schrödinger unitary\n$\\mathcal U:\\mathcal F_s(\\mathbb C^r)\\to L^2(\\mathbb R^r)$ of\n[[lem-nrqmx-finite-mode-fock-schr-identification]]. In Schrödinger coordinates\ndefine the self-adjoint quadratures\n$$Q_j=\\mathcal U^{-1}(\\sqrt\\hbar\\,x_j)\\mathcal U,\\qquad P_j=\\mathcal U^{-1}(-i\\sqrt\\hbar\\,\\partial_{x_j})\\mathcal U,$$\nwhere $x_j$ is the real multiplication operator on its maximal domain and\n$-i\\partial_{x_j}$ is defined by conjugating that multiplication operator by\nthe unitary Fourier transform. Their common Schwartz core satisfies\n$$[Q_j,Q_k]=[P_j,P_k]=0,\\qquad [Q_j,P_k]=i\\hbar\\delta_{jk}I.$$\nWrite $R=(Q_1,P_1,\\ldots,Q_r,P_r)^T$ and\n$J=\\bigoplus_{j=1}^r\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\-1&0\\end{pmatrix}$.\nThe Weyl unitary labelled by $z=(q_1,p_1,\\ldots,q_r,p_r)$ is the unitary\nwhose grouped-coordinate formula, with $q=(q_1,\\ldots,q_r)$ and\n$p=(p_1,\\ldots,p_r)$, is\n$$[W(z)f](x)=e^{i\\sqrt\\hbar\\,q\\cdot(x+\\sqrt\\hbar p/2)}f(x+\\sqrt\\hbar p).$$\nIt obeys $W(z)W(z')=e^{-i\\hbar z^TJz'/2}W(z+z')$ and is regular and\nirreducible. By the finite-dimensional Stone–von Neumann theorem, every\nnonzero irreducible regular Weyl system with this finite phase space and this\nsame nonzero central parameter is unitarily equivalent to it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix an integer $n\\ge1$. With the complex smooth-function convention of [[def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions]] and multi-indices of [[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]], define\n$$p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)=\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb R^n}|x^\\alpha\\partial^\\beta f(x)|,\\qquad \\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^n)=\\{f\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C):p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)<\\infty\\text{ for all }\\alpha,\\beta\\}.$$\nThe zero multi-index gives $x^0=1$ and $\\partial^0f=f$. These are actual smooth functions, not equivalence classes. The notation $C_c^\\infty$ uses the support convention in [[def-c-c-and-c-c-infinity-on-rn]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-fourier-characterisation-of-integer-order-hilbert-sobolev-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "1. $E_k[H^k]=\\{u_f:f\\in W^{k,2}\\}$, and\n   $\\Phi(f):=E_k^{-1}(u_f)$ is a bijection\n   $\\Phi:W^{k,2}\\to H^k$. Thus, under the regular-distribution embedding,\n   $W^{k,2}$ and $H^k$ are the same subspace of $\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^n)$.\n2. There are constants $0<c_k\\le C_k<\\infty$, depending only on $n$ and $k$,\n   with\n$$c_k^{1/2}\\,\\|\\langle\\xi\\rangle^k\\mathcal F_2f\\|_2\\le\\|f\\|_{W^{k,2}} \\le C_k^{1/2}\\,\\|\\langle\\xi\\rangle^k\\mathcal F_2f\\|_2,$$\n   and $\\|\\langle\\xi\\rangle^k\\mathcal F_2f\\|_2=\\|\\Phi(f)\\|_{H^k}$. Hence the\n   weak-derivative norm and the bracket-weighted $L^2$ norm are equivalent,\n   and the latter is also equivalent to\n   $\\|(1+4\\pi^2|\\xi|^2)^{k/2}\\mathcal F_2f\\|_2$, by\n$$\\|\\langle\\xi\\rangle^k h\\|_2\\le\\|(1+4\\pi^2|\\xi|^2)^{k/2}h\\|_2 \\le(2\\pi)^k\\|\\langle\\xi\\rangle^k h\\|_2,\\qquad h\\in L^2 .$$\n3. The three norm expressions are **not** identical in general: the\n   bracket-weighted norm differs from both the weak-derivative norm and the\n   Laplacian-weighted norm, as the Gaussian computation in the proof shows.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $0<r<R$ and $n\\ge 1$. Then there exists a smooth function $\\rho:\\mathbb R^n\\to [0,1]$ such that $\\rho=1$ on $\\overline B_r(0)$ and $\\operatorname{supp}(\\rho)\\subseteq B_R(0)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-weak-leibniz-rule-with-a-smooth-factor",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open with\n$n\\ge1$, let $\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$, and let\n$\\eta\\in C^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$ and\n$u\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$. For\n$\\alpha\\in\\mathbb N_0^n$, suppose that for every $\\gamma\\le\\alpha$ there is\n$v_\\gamma\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$ with\n$D^\\gamma u=v_\\gamma$ weakly. Then $\\eta u$ has a weak $\\alpha$-derivative,\nand its almost-everywhere class is\n$$D^\\alpha(\\eta u)=\\sum_{\\beta\\le\\alpha}\\binom{\\alpha}{\\beta}(D^\\beta\\eta)D^{\\alpha-\\beta}u\\quad\\text{in }L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega;\\mathbb K).$$\nwhere $D^\\beta\\eta$ is the classical derivative and products are pointwise\nproducts of representatives. The resulting class does not depend on the\nrepresentatives.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-compactly-supported-smooth-functions-are-dense-in-wkp-of-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $n\\ge1$, $k\\in\\mathbb N_0$, $1\\le p<\\infty$ and\n$\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$. Then the compactly supported smooth\nfunctions $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb K)$ are dense in\n$W^{k,p}(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb K)$: for every\n$u\\in W^{k,p}(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb K)$ and every $\\delta>0$ there is\n$\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb K)$ with\n$$\\|\\varphi-u\\|_{W^{k,p}(\\mathbb R^n)}<\\delta.$$\nNo such norm-density assertion is made for $p=\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be an arbitrary measure space. All functions below have domain $X$. Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand hence\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Choose $\\chi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^r)$ with $0\\le\\chi\\le1$,\n$\\chi=1$ on $\\overline B_1(0)$, and support in $B_2(0)$ by [F3]. For\n$R\\ge1$ set $\\chi_R(x)=\\chi(x/R)$ and $f_R=\\chi_Rf$. Then\n$|\\partial_j\\chi_R|\\le C/R$. By [F4], $f_R\\in W^{1,2}$ and\n$$\\partial_j(f_R-f)=(\\chi_R-1)\\partial_jf+(\\partial_j\\chi_R)f.$$\nThe first terms in $f_R-f$, $x_j(f_R-f)$, and\n$(\\chi_R-1)\\partial_jf$ tend to zero in $L^2$ by dominated convergence [F6],\nsince $\\chi_R\\to1$ pointwise and $|\\chi_R-1|\\le1$. The remaining derivative\nterm has norm at most $C\\|f\\|_2/R$. Thus $\\|f_R-f\\|_{\\mathrm{quad}}\\to0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Fix $R$ so the preceding error is below $\\varepsilon/2$. The function\n$f_R$ is supported in $\\overline B_{2R}(0)$. By [F5], choose\n$\\phi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^r)$ with $\\|\\phi-f_R\\|_{W^{1,2}}$ as small\nas needed. Choose $\\theta\\in C_c^\\infty$ equal to $1$ on\n$\\overline B_{2R}(0)$ and supported in $B_{2R+2}(0)$ by [F3], and put\n$g=\\theta\\phi$. Since $\\theta f_R=f_R$, the weak product rule [F4] gives\n$$g-f_R=\\theta(\\phi-f_R),\\qquad\n\\partial_j(g-f_R)=\\theta\\partial_j(\\phi-f_R)+(partial_j\\theta)(\\phi-f_R).$$\nThe bounded derivatives of $\\theta$ make the $W^{1,2}$ norm of $g-f_R$ at\nmost a fixed constant times $\\|\\phi-f_R\\|_{W^{1,2}}$. Both $g$ and $f_R$\nare supported in $\\overline B_{2R+2}(0)$, so\n$$\\|x_j(g-f_R)\\|_2\\le(2R+2)\\|g-f_R\\|_2.$$\nChoose $\\phi$ close enough that $\\|g-f_R\\|_{\\mathrm{quad}}<\\varepsilon/2$.\nThen $g\\in C_c^\\infty\\subset\\mathcal S$ and\n$\\|g-f\\|_{\\mathrm{quad}}<\\varepsilon$ by step 1.1. Since $f$ and\n$\\varepsilon>0$ were arbitrary, $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^r)$ is dense in $D$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
